ernative =0Ato stocks after many years when governments essentially forced =
them to take a =0Arisk. But the process needs to be slow so that all the pl=
ayers can adjust. The =0Aspeedy backup in yields we are now witnessing isn=
=E2=80=99t without danger.=0A=0AIt=E2=80=99s hard to impute what=E2=80=99s =
happening to inflation, because the picture for =0Aprice rises looks no wor=
se, and arguably somewhat more reassuring, than it did =0Abefore yields beg=
an to rise in earnest. Core PCE (Personal Consumption =0AExpenditure) infla=
tion remains at 3%, right at the top of the Fed=E2=80=99s zone of =0Atolera=
nce, according to August numbers released Wednesday. But this is driven =0A=
by outliers. A core version derived from the trimmed mean - excluding extre=
mes =0Ain both directions - continued to decline and is now at only 2.3%. T=
here=E2=80=99s no =0Agreat reason for fear in that:=0A=0AIt=E2=80=99s also =
hard to attribute this to any great loss of confidence in the US, =0Awhatev=
er many foreigners think of President Donald Trump and hisattempts to =0Abu=
