big rise in borrowing =0Acosts. Even as German bunds rallied, the yield on =
10-year OATS rose to 4.92%. =0AIf it tops 5%, it will be the first time sin=
ce 2002, when France=E2=80=99s finances and =0Aits politics were not in suc=
h disarray:=0A=0AAs France is part of the euro zone, any crisis must natura=
lly extend to the =0Arest of the currency area, at a time when most of its =
members are moving in a =0Anationalist direction and likely reluctant to he=
lp. That is beginning to have =0Aan impact on stocks. The main European bou=
rses sold off even as US equities =0Arallied on Thursday. In combination wi=
th a weakening euro, the continent=E2=80=99s =0Astocks have now given up al=
most all their gains since Trump took office:=0A=0AWhat next=3F The history=
 of the last 25 year suggests a) the European Central =0ABank will interven=
e to put a lid on yields - which may be politically easier if =0APresident =
Christine Lagarde, who is French, does indeed leave early, and b) =0Athat E=
uropean politicians will find a way to muddle through. There will be =0Aple=
nty of opportunities for hedge funds and other traders to make money on the=
 =0Atwists and turns ahead, but the likelihood is that this crisis will be =
resolved =0Alike the last - by kicking the can down the road.=0A=0AIf there=
=E2=80=99s an added element, it=E2=80=99s political. The euro zone=E2=80=99=
s structure, with =0Aindependent fiscal policy but a common currency, and a=
 central bank that =0Aultimately will do what it takes to keep the euro pro=
ject alive, is evidently =0Aflawed. We=E2=80=99ve known that for a while. U=
ntil now, there has been no political =0Aappetite to find out what would ha=
ppen if we just gave up on that model.=0A=0APopulist politicians aren=E2=80=
=99t necessarily bent on self-destruction, as the =0Aextremely pragmatic Gi=
orgia Meloni has demonstrated in Italy. But the big risk =0Ais that voters =
in one euro-zone member or another decide to blow it all up. =0AThat=E2=80=
=99s arguably more likely in Germany than France, but in the medium term it=
 =0Ais political decisions that could transform yet another example of pain=
fully =0Aslow European growth into something worse. The spark for the crisi=
s was lit in =0Athe US; European voters get to decide whether it detonates.=
